---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import BitTokenDiagram from '../components/BitTokenDiagram.astro';
import DotPlot from '../components/DotPlot.astro';
import LoopDiagram from '../components/LoopDiagram.astro';
import RoundsInspector from '../components/RoundsInspector.astro';
import { current, experimentById, progress } from '../lib/data';
import { pct, thousands } from '../lib/format';

const arch = current.architecture;
const tokens = arch.kind === 'bittoken';
const layerName = tokens ? 'bit layers' : 'look layers';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const share = (n: number) => `${((100 * n) / arch.parameters).toFixed(1)}%`;
const bitTokens = progress.architectures.bittoken;
const capacityRuns = [
  { id: 'nearonly', name: 'loop', color: 'var(--series-2)' },
  { id: 'nearonly-scratch4', name: 'loop + scratch slots', color: 'var(--series-4)' },
  { id: 'nearonly-bittoken', name: 'bit tokens', color: 'var(--series-1)' },
];
const capacityRelations = ['a*b+b', 'a*b+5', 'a*b-a', '(a*b)^a', 'a*b+a'];
const capacityExact = (id: string, name: string) => experimentById(id)?.families[name]?.exact ?? null;
---

<Layout title="Model" description="The bit-token design: 999 examples encoded once, one token per answer bit, the guess refined over rounds with the same weights.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Model</p>
  <h1>How the bit-token model works</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    It is never told which relation it faces. It reads the 999 examples once, then <strong>guesses every bit of the
    answer and corrects the guess</strong>, round after round, by looking back at the examples. The same weights run
    every round.
  </p>
  <p class="small muted">
    This page shows the 8-bit version, which is small enough to inspect. The width-free model on the overview uses the
    same design with inputs that do not depend on the width, so the same weights answer 8- to 32-bit questions
    (939K parameters; <code>src/teenie/bits/wide_model.py</code>).
  </p>

  <div class="grid-3 stats">
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">Parameters</span>
      <span class="value">{thousands(arch.parameters)}</span>
      <span class="note">about {Math.round(arch.parameters / 1000)}K — all of them reused every round</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">Layers per round · rounds</span>
      <span class="value">{arch.layers} · {arch.rounds}</span>
      <span class="note">
        {`${arch.layers} ${layerName} (${arch.heads} attention heads each), repeated for ${arch.rounds} rounds`}
      </span>
    </div>
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">Width</span>
      <span class="value">{arch.hidden}</span>
      <span class="note">hidden size; numbers are {arch.width}-bit, context fixed at 999 examples</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Architecture</h2>
  <div class="card">
    {
      tokens ? (
        <BitTokenDiagram layers={arch.layers} rounds={arch.rounds} heads={arch.heads} />
      ) : (
        <LoopDiagram layers={arch.layers} rounds={arch.rounds} heads={arch.heads} scratch={arch.scratch} />
      )
    }
  </div>

  <div class="grid-2 steps">
    <div class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">1 · Read the examples once</p>
      <p>
        Each example's 24 bits (<code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>c</code>) go through a small encoder whose first
        layer is <strong>bilinear</strong> — two projections multiplied together — and become one vector. The 999
        vectors stay fixed for every round: no reading order, no direction to choose.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">2 · Hold a guess</p>
      <p>
        The question starts with all 8 answer bits undecided. Its input layer is bilinear too, so products of its own
        bits such as <code>a<sub>i</sub>·b<sub>j</sub></code> — the pieces of a multiplication — are one step away.
        {tokens && ' The question is split into 8 tokens, one per answer bit, each knowing which bit it is.'}
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">3 · Look and correct</p>
      <p>
        {
          tokens
            ? `Each round, in each of ${arch.layers} bit layers, the bit tokens first attend to each other — so carries and partial products can move between bit positions — then each reads the 999 example vectors, then a gated (SwiGLU) layer that can multiply signals updates them. A memory per token carries to the next round, and each token writes its own bit of the new guess.`
            : `Each round the question, carrying its current guess and memory, attends to the 999 example vectors in ${arch.layers} look layers, each followed by a gated (SwiGLU) layer that can multiply signals instead of only adding them.${arch.scratch > 0 ? ` It then exchanges information with its ${arch.scratch} scratch slots — memory carried between rounds and never scored, for intermediate results.` : ''} A GRU cell updates the memory and the readout writes a new guess.`
        }
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow">4 · Answer honestly</p>
      <p>
        {`After round ${arch.rounds} every bit is rounded to 0 or 1. Confidence is the certainty of the least certain bit: on relations it has never seen it seldom claims to be sure, and when it does it is right ${pct(current.summary.hidden?.sure_accuracy)} of the time on hidden compositions.`}
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Where the parameters are</h2>
  <div class="card table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>part</th>
          <th>what it does</th>
          <th class="num">parameters</th>
          <th class="num">share</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          arch.parts.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <b>{p.part}</b>
              </td>
              <td class="muted">{p.detail}</td>
              <td class="num">{thousands(p.parameters)}</td>
              <td class="num">{share(p.parameters)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
        <tr>
          <td><b>Total</b></td>
          <td></td>
          <td class="num"><b>{thousands(arch.parameters)}</b></td>
          <td class="num">100%</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Watch it answer</h2>
  <p>
    Recorded from the current model on fresh episodes. Play through the rounds: the guess starts undecided and
    sharpens as the question keeps looking back at the examples. <code>add</code> and <code>max</code> settle almost
    immediately; {`a*b takes longer (${pct(current.families['a*b'])} exact for this model);`}
    <code>a*b+a</code> and the hidden sum loop show where it still fails.
  </p>
  <RoundsInspector />

  {
    bitTokens && !tokens && (
      <section class="next">
        <h2>In testing: bit tokens</h2>
        <p>
          The current model holds each question as one vector. That is enough to multiply, and enough to add — but not
          to do one after the other: a product followed by another step needs carries to move between bit positions
          through an intermediate result. The bit-token model gives every answer bit its own token. Each round the bit
          tokens attend to each other, each reads the 999 examples, and a gated layer updates them.
        </p>
        <div class="grid-3 stats">
          <div class="card stat">
            <span class="label">Parameters</span>
            <span class="value">{thousands(bitTokens.parameters)}</span>
            <span class="note">{`about ${Math.round(bitTokens.parameters / 1000)}K at width ${bitTokens.hidden}`}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="card stat">
            <span class="label">Layers per round · rounds</span>
            <span class="value">{`${bitTokens.layers} · ${bitTokens.rounds}`}</span>
            <span class="note">{`${bitTokens.layers} bit layers (${bitTokens.heads} heads), 8 tokens per question`}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="card stat">
            <span class="label">"Product, then a step" (trained on it alone)</span>
            <span class="value">{pct(capacityExact('nearonly-bittoken', 'a*b+b'))}</span>
            <span class="note">{`the current design reaches ${pct(capacityExact('nearonly', 'a*b+b'))} with the same training`}</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <BitTokenDiagram layers={bitTokens.layers} rounds={bitTokens.rounds} heads={bitTokens.heads} />
        </div>
        <h3>Can it compute a product followed by another step?</h3>
        <p class="small">
          Capacity check: each design trained on nothing but these four relations (3,000 updates), then tested on fresh
          questions. <code>a*b+a</code> was never shown to any of them.
        </p>
        <div class="card">
          <DotPlot
            title="Exact match after training only on product-then-step relations"
            reference={0.5}
            labelWidth={150}
            methods={capacityRuns.map((r) => ({ name: r.name, color: r.color }))}
            rows={capacityRelations.map((name) => ({
              name: name === 'a*b+a' ? 'a*b+a (unseen)' : name,
              values: capacityRuns.map((r) => capacityExact(r.id, name)),
            }))}
          />
        </div>
        <p class="small muted">
          Bit tokens reach 99–100% where a single question vector stalls below 50% — even with scratch memory. Trained on
          only four relations it forces the unseen <code>a*b+a</code> into one it knows, so the real test is broad
          training: it is now training with step-by-step supervision on the full data set, and replaces the current model
          if it wins on the ladder.
        </p>
        <details>
          <summary>Where its parameters are</summary>
          <div class="table-scroll">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr><th>part</th><th>what it does</th><th class="num">parameters</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {bitTokens.parts.map((p) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td><b>{p.part}</b></td>
                    <td class="muted">{p.detail}</td>
                    <td class="num">{thousands(p.parameters)}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
                <tr><td><b>Total</b></td><td></td><td class="num"><b>{thousands(bitTokens.parameters)}</b></td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </details>
      </section>
    )
  }

  {
    tokens && (
      <section class="next">
        <h2>Why one token per answer bit</h2>
        <p>
          The previous model held each question as one vector. It could multiply and it could add, but not one after the
          other: a product followed by another step needs carries to move between bit positions through an intermediate
          result. Trained on nothing but such relations, each design reached:
        </p>
        <div class="card">
          <DotPlot
            title="Exact match after training only on product-then-step relations"
            reference={0.5}
            labelWidth={150}
            methods={capacityRuns.map((r) => ({ name: r.name, color: r.color }))}
            rows={capacityRelations.map((name) => ({
              name: name === 'a*b+a' ? 'a*b+a (unseen)' : name,
              values: capacityRuns.map((r) => capacityExact(r.id, name)),
            }))}
          />
        </div>
      </section>
    )
  }

  <h2>How it is trained</h2>
  <div class="grid-2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Never the same problem twice</h3>
      <p class="small">
        Each step draws 8 fresh episodes: a random relation, 999 new examples and 16 new questions. Relations include
        the named ones, <code>a*b</code>, random expressions of 1–5 operations, loops whose repeat count comes from
        <code>b</code>, and bit loops. Certain operation combinations and loop bodies are never generated, so they can be
        tested as truly unseen.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Effort where it is still wrong</h3>
      <p class="small">
        Every 500 updates the model is checked on each relation kind, and the next episodes are drawn in proportion to how
        often it is still wrong. That alone took multiplication from 45% to 99.6% in an earlier mixed run.
        {` This model: ${arch.updates?.toLocaleString('en-US')} updates on ${arch.train_set}, per-bit cross-entropy, AdamW.`}
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">
    {tokens ? 'Source: src/teenie/bits/bit_token_model.py (--architecture bittoken).' : 'Source: src/teenie/bits/loop_model.py (--architecture loop --multiplicative).'} Results on
    the difficulty ladder: <a href={`${base}/results/`}>Results</a>. How it got here, including the first bit-by-bit
    model: <a href={`${base}/changelog/`}>Changelog</a>.
  </p>
</Layout>

<style>
  .stats {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .steps {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .steps p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .next .stats {
    margin: 1rem 0;
  }
</style>
